Promises start executing immediately when they are created.
They cannot be cancelled and they resolve only once.
Calling .then() multiple times does not re-execute the Promise; it only reads the same resolved value.

Observables start executing only when a subscription happens.
Each subscription starts a new execution (unicast by default).
Observables can emit multiple values over time and can be unsubscribed, which stops execution.

Compared to a normal function, an Observable supports cancellation, asynchronous streams, and powerful operators for transforming data.

in this code  , we can understand how the multiple value emited, for single subscribe

 const obser = new Observable((observer) => {
      console.log('Observable executor running');
      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        observer.next(i);
      }
    });

    obser.subscribe((va) => {
      console.log('from obervable', va);
    });

    const prm = new Promise((resolver) => {
      console.log('promise started');
      for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        resolver(j);
      }
    });

    prm.then((val) => console.log('from promise', val));

Output:
Observable executor running
from obervable 0
from obervable 1
from obervable 2
promise started
from promise 0

| Feature                      | Promise     | Observable        |
| ---------------------------- | ----------- | ----------------- |
| When execution starts        | On creation | On subscribe      |
| How many times producer runs | Once        | Per subscription  |
| Values                       | One         | Many              |
| Over time                    | ❌           | ✅                 |
| Cancel                       | ❌           | ✅ (`unsubscribe`) |
| Operators                    | ❌           | ✅                 |
| Lazy execution               | ❌           | ✅                 |
